# 🏗️ AWS Three-Tier Web Architecture

A production-grade three-tier web application architecture designed and deployed on AWS — built independently to demonstrate real-world cloud infrastructure skills.

---

## 📐 Architecture Overview

```
         [ Users / Internet ]
                 │
         [ Application Load Balancer ]
                 │
        ┌────────┴────────┐
   [ Web Tier ]     [ App Tier ]
   EC2 Instances    EC2 Instances
        └────────┬────────┘
                 │
          [ Data Tier ]
          RDS / Database
```

**Tier 1 — Presentation Layer (Web Tier)**
- EC2 instances serving the frontend
- Sits behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB)
- Public-facing subnet within VPC

**Tier 2 — Application Layer (App Tier)**
- Business logic hosted on EC2
- Private subnet — not directly accessible from the internet
- Communicates only with Web Tier and Data Tier

**Tier 3 — Data Layer (Database Tier)**
- Isolated in a private subnet
- Accessible only from the App Tier
- Secured via Security Groups and IAM policies

---

## ☁️ AWS Services Used

| Service | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **EC2** | Web and App tier compute instances |
| **VPC** | Isolated network with public & private subnets |
| **ALB** | Load balancing traffic across Web Tier |
| **Security Groups** | Firewall rules between tiers |
| **IAM** | Role-based access control |
| **Route Tables** | Routing between subnets and internet |

---

## 🔐 Security Design

- Web Tier only accepts traffic from ALB
- App Tier only accepts traffic from Web Tier Security Group
- Database Tier only accepts traffic from App Tier Security Group
- No direct public access to App or Data tiers
- IAM roles follow least-privilege principle

---

## 🚀 What I Learned

- Designing multi-tier architecture with proper network isolation
- Configuring VPC with public/private subnets and route tables
- Setting up ALB with target groups and health checks
- Applying security group chaining across tiers
- Understanding real-world cloud deployment patterns

